<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Han</h1><p class="st-meta">951 CE – 979 CE · capital Taiyuan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last of the Ten Kingdoms, a Liao-backed Shatuo state at Taiyuan that held out against the Song until 979.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a> in 951, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chong">Liu Chong</a>, brother of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a> and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedong-shanxi">Hedong</a>, proclaimed himself emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, refusing to recognise the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> and claiming to continue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Northern Han held only the poor, mountainous centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> and depended on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, whose emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chong">Liu Chong</a> addressed as 'uncle' and paid tribute. Its army was strong in defence, but it could not afford long wars. With Khitan help it attacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> in 954 and was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaoping">Gaoping</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 968 and 969, even flooding the city, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> relief armies saved it. In 979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-song">Emperor Taizong of Song</a> defeated the Khitan relief force and besieged Taiyuan until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-jiyuan">Liu Jiyuan</a> surrendered. The city was razed, completing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-han.jpg" alt="Northern Han" width="320" height="440"><figcaption>Image: Wangyefurichard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Han_Kingdom,_951.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>951 CE – 979 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taiyuan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Chong, Liu Jun, Liu Jiyuan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Han (Taiyuan), Eastern Han (Five Dynasties)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=965&amp;focus=northern-han" aria-label="Northern Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7487 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7807 1072L7901 1174M7807 1072L7866 1006M7807 1072L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0M7866 1006h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Taiyuan. <a href="/atlas?year=965&amp;focus=northern-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
